WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.391 --> 00:00:13.310
Today's featured speaker is Greg Valiant.

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:17.440
Gregory Valiant is an assistant professor
in Stanford's computer science department.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.410
Some of his recent projects focus
on designing algorithms for

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:23.710
accurately inferring information
about complex distributions

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.990
when given surprisingly little data.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.450
More broadly,
his research interests are in algorithms,

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:32.760
learning, applied probability,
statistics, and evolution.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.180
Prior to joining Stanford, Dr.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:37.400
Valiant was a postdoc at
the Microsoft Lab in New England.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.402
Which focused on integrating
mathematical and algorithmic

00:00:40.402 --> 00:00:44.940
sciences with both the social sciences and
aspects of the biomedical sciences.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.786
He received a PhD from UC Berkeley in
Computer Science and degree from Harvard.

00:00:49.786 --> 00:00:52.236
He currently teaches CS 161 design and

00:00:52.236 --> 00:00:56.170
analysis of algorithms this
winter quarter at Stanford.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:00.640
Which is part of our SCPD portfolio
offered to our industry students.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.940
So now,
I'd like to turn the floor over to Greg.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.380
&gt;&gt; Okay, well thanks Stephanie,
and thanks for logging in.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:09.572
So today I was going to tell you
about a few perspectives on how

00:01:09.572 --> 00:01:13.278
to get a surprising amount of
information from a given amount of data.

00:01:17.408 --> 00:01:21.770
So, a sort of mindset that underpins
this work is the following question.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.790
So, if you're given some data that's
drawn from a very complex distribution.

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:27.970
So, what do you mean by complex?

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:30.990
Well, maybe it's a very high
dimensional distribution.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.070
Maybe if the distribution with
very large domain or alphabet,

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:36.850
it may be a distribution
over rare genetic mutations.

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:40.640
Or maybe it's a distribution
with very complex dependencies,

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:41.800
such as natural language.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.860
Then, and oftentimes,
even if you have a lot of data,

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:50.900
the empirical distribution
of the data is misleading.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.610
In the case of high
dimensional distributions,

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:57.642
well because of the dimensionality,
you need an enormous amount of data

00:01:57.642 --> 00:02:00.170
to kind of fill out
the geometry of the space.

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:03.414
In the case of distributions
over very large domains,

00:02:03.414 --> 00:02:08.067
often even when you have lots of data you
still fail to observe some of the domain

00:02:08.067 --> 00:02:12.700
elements that do arise with
non-negligible probabilities.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:17.760
In the case of complex dependencies,
well even when you have lots of data,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.820
sometimes you don't actually see
all of these complex dependencies.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.150
So in these cases,

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:28.110
the empirical distribution of the data
you have is often misleading.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.530
And the basic question is, well,
in this undersampled regime,

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.960
can we still recover accurate information
about the underlying distributions?

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.550
What can you say about the unseen
portion of the distribution?

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.250
Can you say anything about
that domain elements that you

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.290
haven't actually observed?

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:49.250
Or in cases where maybe you can't assume
great answers to the above two questions

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:52.020
could you still estimate
the value of collecting more data?

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.293
Could you estimate well maybe if I
were to get 10 times more data or

00:02:55.293 --> 00:02:58.516
100 times more data I would be
able to answer these questions.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.440
So I want to give two motivations for
this work.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:07.510
So the first is that even though
today's machine learning systems

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:12.140
are extremely impressive, currently
they still use the data they have

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:17.930
extremely inefficiently to just
to give one concrete data point.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.990
So humans learn to speak after
hearing roughly 50 million words.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:23.750
So between when you are born and
when you are three or

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:28.470
four or five years old, most people
hear 50 million words spoken to them.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.700
So again, this isn't distinct words.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:32.869
This is 50 million utterances of words.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:38.120
And so after hearing these 50 million
words, most children have a good grasp of

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:43.400
grammar, good grasp of semantics and
they basically speak like humans.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:49.733
In contrast, even the state of the art
natural language processing systems today,

00:03:49.733 --> 00:03:54.294
they're fed the entire Wikipedia corpus
over and over and over and over again.

00:03:54.294 --> 00:03:59.610
And at the end of the day they still lack
some basic grammatical understanding.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.110
And lack kind of sense of semantics.

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.400
Okay, so even though today's machine
learning systems are very, very

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.950
impressive, they don't use information
that they have all that efficiently.

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:14.500
The second motivation I wanted to
mention is this apparent paradox that

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:16.760
as the number of datasets
around us have grown and

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:20.790
as the sizes of these datasets have grown,
in fact we are increasingly faced

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:24.630
with this problem of seeing these
under sample distributions.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:29.045
And in the next five to ten years I think
we're going to start to see many more

00:04:29.045 --> 00:04:33.531
advances that are based upon getting
more information from the given data,

00:04:33.531 --> 00:04:37.272
getting more information from
the data that we actually have.

00:04:40.504 --> 00:04:44.510
So with these motivations in mind, I
wanted to discuss three sort of different

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:47.650
settings that I've been
working on recently.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:52.392
These three settings will all have
the property that for a given amount of

00:04:52.392 --> 00:04:56.979
data even when the empirical
distribution of the data is misleading,

00:04:56.979 --> 00:05:02.439
we'll still be able to accurately say
things about the underlying distribution.

00:05:02.439 --> 00:05:04.679
So in the first part,
which is sort of a warm up,

00:05:04.679 --> 00:05:08.043
we'll consider accepting where there
are lots and lots of people, but

00:05:08.043 --> 00:05:11.030
each person only has
a tiny amount of data.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:14.200
And we'll see that you can leverage
the fact that there are lots and

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:18.980
lots of people to improve your empirical
understanding of each person even

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:20.900
though they only have
a little bit of data.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:26.510
For the second part of the talk we'll
consider a setting where we have a sample

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:29.645
drawn from a distribution over
an extremely large domain.

00:05:29.645 --> 00:05:33.832
That we'll actually be able to accurately
infer the structure of the portion of

00:05:33.832 --> 00:05:36.530
the domain that we haven't
seen any samples from.

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:38.731
So we'll actually be able to infer things,
but

00:05:38.731 --> 00:05:42.485
the number of unseen domain elements
that arise with different probabilities.

00:05:44.536 --> 00:05:47.790
In the last part of the talk,
we'll consider geometric setting.

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:50.810
So we're given data drawn from some
high dimensional distribution.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:56.110
And we'll see that even when we have too
little data to understand the geometric

00:05:56.110 --> 00:06:00.410
structure of the distribution, so
we'll have too little data to say,

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:02.990
these are the big principal
components of the distribution.

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:06.630
We'll be able to describe
what structure is there.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:10.810
So we'll say, we don't know what
the principle components are but

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:14.170
we know that there are 5
directions of big variants and

00:06:14.170 --> 00:06:15.980
50 directions of medium variants and
so on.

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:20.790
So while all three of these settings
are going to be rather different,

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:23.090
and the results will be
different in these settings,

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:25.830
there's a common perspective
that underlies and

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:28.610
enables the algorithms that we'll
see in these different settings.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:31.424
Okay, so let's start.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:36.660
So in this first setting,
imagine you have n people, and

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:39.060
think of n as being very large.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:42.940
And each person has some
hidden parameter p sub i.

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:47.447
Maybe p sub i is the probability that
the i-th person gets the flu each year or

00:06:47.447 --> 00:06:52.167
maybe p sub i is the probability that each
person pays their credit card late each

00:06:52.167 --> 00:06:53.591
month or changes jobs.

00:06:53.591 --> 00:06:56.956
Or maybe p sub i is some biological
traits, though maybe p sub i

00:06:56.956 --> 00:07:00.732
is probability that a person has male
versus a female child and so on.

00:07:02.765 --> 00:07:08.220
So imagine we have a data set, so we have
lots and lots of people, we have n people.

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:13.430
However, each person we only say, in this
case, observe two flips of their coin,

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:19.560
so we observe two independent random
variables for each person with,

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:25.430
or yes with probability p sub i and
no with probability 1- p sub i.

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:29.130
In this case, what can we hope to
say about each person's probability?

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:34.440
Well, the first thing to observe
is that the empirical estimates

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.450
of the p i's are extremely unreliable.

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:39.612
We just have two flips
of each person's coin.

00:07:39.612 --> 00:07:40.680
What can we hope to say?

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:44.270
So if we look at the first person,
they flipped their coin twice.

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:45.810
They got two yeses.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:49.380
The empirical estimate of this
first person's probability is 1.

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:53.250
In both tosses they got yes,
empirical estimate is 1.

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:57.580
And obviously, this is very noisy because
we only have two flips of the coin.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:02.440
Okay, so in general, if you want to
estimate the bias of a coin to error

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:06.410
epsilon, you do need to flip it roughly
one over epsilon squared times.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:09.060
It's here we only have two tosses.

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:10.070
What can we hope to say?

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:15.900
Well, suppose we look at all
the data in aggregate, and

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:18.700
we happen to know just
the following phenomenon.

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:22.830
That suppose we see that actually
the number of people who got two yes' is

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:26.890
roughly equal to the number of people who
got two no's, which is equal to the number

00:08:26.890 --> 00:08:31.950
of people who got a no and a yes, a yes
and a no, and these are very accurately,

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:36.540
the number of people with each of
these is very close to and over four.

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:41.770
So I claim that based on,
if we work out seamless data,

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:45.300
we can actually conclude that
almost all of these probabilities,

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:48.320
almost all of the pizza buys,
most be extremely close to a half.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:52.312
So what's the reasoning behind this?

00:08:52.312 --> 00:08:57.158
Well, suppose that 10% of the people
had a probability of point six and

00:08:57.158 --> 00:08:59.660
10% had a probability of point four and

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:01.690
maybe the rest of the people had
a probability of point five.

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:04.970
Then the theme that we
would actually start to

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:07.870
see a noticeable amount of
correlation in these two flips.

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:10.698
We'd actually see more
people with the yes yes and

00:09:10.698 --> 00:09:14.547
more people with the no no then with
the mixed no yes' and yes no's.

00:09:16.803 --> 00:09:22.459
So if you think about this a little bit,
if we actually saw data like this,

00:09:22.459 --> 00:09:28.299
I claim that we could really conclude
that at least N minus to the two-thirds

00:09:28.299 --> 00:09:35.080
of these UPI's are equal to a half plus or
minus maybe 1 over the cube root of N.

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:37.570
And so we can make extremely concrete

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:40.590
statements about this that really
pin down the of p sub i's.

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:46.230
And again, the intuition is that if
this wasn't the case, if there was more

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:49.770
variation between the Pi's, we would have
seen very different summary statistics.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.490
Okay, so this is pretty amazing.

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:58.710
So suppose we solved,
we looked at the aggregate statistics and

00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:02.000
saw almost the exact,
n/4 people with each of these outcomes.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:08.590
And based on this we concluded that almost
all of these Pi's are very close to half.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:14.260
And now suppose you said, look at
this fourth person, they got two nos.

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:16.350
What do you suppose their
true probability is?

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:17.860
What do you think p sub 4 is?

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:23.960
Well, now their argument would be,
look almost all of the Pi's are half, so

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:26.480
I think p sub 4 is probably
very close to a half.

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:31.840
And on average, this will be very good.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:37.270
We will get extremely low average error
over our estimates of all the people.

00:10:37.270 --> 00:10:38.620
And this is pretty surprising, right?

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:42.100
So if you think about what's
going on here we're estimating

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:45.170
P sub 4 more accurately than
empirical distribution.

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:50.630
Based on using information about
the independent random variables

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:52.800
corresponding to everybody
else's coin tosses.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:57.512
So we're estimating p4,
not just based upon the tosses of the coin

00:10:57.512 --> 00:11:02.390
that land heads is called op4, but
also based upon the probability of

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:06.936
it landing hands of all these other
independent A random variable

00:11:06.936 --> 00:11:11.590
corresponding to different people,
this is pretty surprising.

00:11:14.122 --> 00:11:19.431
So you might wonder to what extent have
I cherry picked the special settings,

00:11:19.431 --> 00:11:24.760
where exactly n over 4, the people
answered yes, yes, no, no, and so on.

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:30.020
So it turns out that even in
the worst case no matter what

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:35.220
the set of underlying Pi's are you can
accurately learn the set of Pi's to

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:40.350
average our epsilon using only 1 over
epsilon samples from each individual.

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:42.880
Only 1 over epsilon opposite of each coin.

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:46.480
Again as opposed to if you were to
naively use the empirical estimate.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:48.710
Which you do require a 1 over epsilon
squared tosses of each point.

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:53.250
So this is a very simple setting.

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:56.750
We're just trying to estimate
the steps of biases of coins

00:11:56.750 --> 00:11:59.540
that rises in many settings, very useful.

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:04.900
But there are lots of more
A higher level analogue

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:07.940
to the setting which we're considering and
which we are thinking about now.

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:10.470
So there are these more
complex distributions.

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:13.830
So instead of just each
person having one coin,

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:18.730
imagine each person has something
that happens as probability p sub i,

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:21.670
something else that might happen
as probability q sub i, and so on.

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:26.110
So maybe, each person corresponds
to a visit or to a website.

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:30.820
P sub i is whether they buy an item,
Q sub i is whether they end up

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:36.710
asking for
something on the help page and so on.

00:12:36.710 --> 00:12:39.454
So maybe, there are these more
complicated distributions,

00:12:39.454 --> 00:12:41.995
to what extent can we get
an analogous results for these.

00:12:44.029 --> 00:12:47.618
You might also wonder how you can leverage
additional structure to the problem to be

00:12:47.618 --> 00:12:49.050
able to do even better.

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:51.660
So these are some avenues that
we're currently thinking about.

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:59.010
So just to summarize, the punch lines
of some of the first part of the talk.

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:01.980
So even with very little
data from each person

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:04.430
We can accurately recover the stats or
population

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:08.000
of distributions by leveraging the fact
that we have data from lots of people.

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:14.330
And as we saw, so in many cases, we can
then use this information to go back and

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:17.900
improve our estimates of
individual people's distributions.

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:20.540
This is the example where
first we concluded that

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:23.740
almost all of the p sub bias
must be very close to half and

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:27.580
then we go back and say well the fourth
person even though the empirical estimate

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:31.810
looks like they're a probability of
0 their piece is also probably half.

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:37.690
And you can do this as a special case
as what's known as Stein's Phenomena.

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:40.880
So this is named after
the late Charles Stein who was

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:44.290
a statistician at Stanford and
passed away a couple months ago.

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:48.110
He was the first one who noticed that you
can actually improve estimates of some

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:53.780
parameters based on observing
independent random variables.

00:13:53.780 --> 00:13:57.020
So even though I'm trying to estimate For

00:13:57.020 --> 00:14:00.610
the fourth person's probability,
I can improve my estimates of this,

00:14:00.610 --> 00:14:04.600
on average, if I look at these
other independent random variables.

00:14:06.580 --> 00:14:09.740
So now we'll transition into the second
part where we'll talk about how to

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:14.539
accurately estimate properties of
the unseen portion of a distribution.

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:21.790
So before I go on, let me stress that all
of the techniques that I'm talking about

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:27.970
are ways of cleaning up the empirical
distribution of the samples, and

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:30.440
these are ways of cleaning up
the empirical distribution

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:33.950
without making any prior assumptions
on the distribution in question.

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:37.379
So we're doing this based
purely on an understanding

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:43.065
Of how the randomness from
the sampling process ends up,

00:14:43.065 --> 00:14:50.310
in some sense, noising up the empirical
distribution of what we see.

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:56.100
Okay, so in the second portion, We'll be
talking about settings where we're given

00:14:56.100 --> 00:15:00.000
independent draws from some
distribution over a discrete support.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:03.950
So think about it as we're sampling
species of fish from some population.

00:15:03.950 --> 00:15:07.170
So we are independently
drawing fish from an ocean,

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:10.210
and we should think about this
as we're sampling each fish with

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:14.230
probability proportional to
the probability of that fish in the ocean.

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:19.460
Okay, so in this example, we drew six
fish, and we saw three different species.

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:22.190
What can we hope to say about
the underlying distribution of fish?

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:30.530
Well, the empirical distribution of
the sample, this is the maximum likelihood

00:15:30.530 --> 00:15:34.690
distribution that maximizes likelihood
of drawing exactly what we observed.

00:15:36.410 --> 00:15:39.970
However, it does not capture the
unobserved portion of the distribution.

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:43.550
So in this case,
we saw three different species of fish.

00:15:43.550 --> 00:15:47.010
Maybe there's a fourth species or
a fifth species of fish in the ocean.

00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:49.370
The empirical distribution does
not account for this at all.

00:15:49.370 --> 00:15:52.560
So there are a few natural questions.

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:55.020
So one is,
what could we even hope to infer

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:57.670
about the unobserved portion
of the distribution?

00:15:57.670 --> 00:15:58.900
Can we hope to say anything about it?

00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:01.820
As we'll see the answer is yes.

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:04.600
We can actually say very
nuanced things about this.

00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:09.460
And the second point is well,
how can these instances about unobserved

00:16:09.460 --> 00:16:12.377
proportion of the distribution
yield better estimates for

00:16:12.377 --> 00:16:14.664
aspects of a distribution
that we care about.

00:16:14.664 --> 00:16:18.162
Suppose you want to estimate
the entropy of the distribution or

00:16:18.162 --> 00:16:20.280
other properties of it.

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:25.018
How can we leverage a better understanding
of the unobserved portion to improve these

00:16:25.018 --> 00:16:27.668
estimates over just
the empirical estimates?

00:16:32.997 --> 00:16:37.607
So in some sense the motivation for
my work, or the inspiration for my work on

00:16:37.607 --> 00:16:42.363
these problems goes back to very early
work of R.A. Fisher, and Allen Turing

00:16:42.363 --> 00:16:47.360
who are respectively the godfathers of
modern statistics and computer science.

00:16:49.374 --> 00:16:55.180
So in the case of Fisher, he was given
this data of a biologist named Corbet.

00:16:55.180 --> 00:16:59.510
So Corbet spent a year in the Malaysian
jungle in the 1940s collecting

00:16:59.510 --> 00:17:01.050
butterflies.

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:05.573
And this chart at the bottom of the page,
this is Corbet's Butterfly Data.

00:17:05.573 --> 00:17:07.328
So what does this represent?

00:17:07.328 --> 00:17:12.545
Well, the left-most spike represents
the fact that, while he was in Malaysia,

00:17:12.545 --> 00:17:16.340
he discovered or
he caught 110 species of butterflies.

00:17:17.590 --> 00:17:19.110
And of these 110 species of butterflies,

00:17:19.110 --> 00:17:22.655
he only ever saw individuals from them,
exactly once.

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:28.840
The second spike represents
the fact that there were 75 species

00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:31.140
of butterflies that he
caught exactly twice.

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:36.280
The third spike represents the fact
that were something like 45 species

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:39.210
of butterflies that he caught three
times in his year in Malaysia.

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:43.770
And way off on the right of the plot,
which is cut off.

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:47.804
Yes, there's the common ground moth, and
you probably caught that 1,000 times.

00:17:47.804 --> 00:17:50.241
And that corresponds to
a spike of height 1,

00:17:50.241 --> 00:17:54.486
representing the fact that there's
1 species that he saw 1,000 times.

00:17:54.486 --> 00:17:58.886
So this would be a spike at
location 1,000 of height 1.

00:17:58.886 --> 00:18:04.629
So while he was there he discovered
something like 230 species of butterflies.

00:18:04.629 --> 00:18:09.383
So he sent his data to Ari Fisher,
who's the big statistician of the time,

00:18:09.383 --> 00:18:10.673
and he said listen.

00:18:10.673 --> 00:18:14.331
I'm thinking about staying for
another year in the jungle,

00:18:14.331 --> 00:18:17.988
can you tell me how many new
species you think I would discover

00:18:17.988 --> 00:18:22.098
if I catch butterflies at the same
rate as I did during my first year.

00:18:22.098 --> 00:18:27.592
And it actually turns out that there is
a extremely accurate estimate for this.

00:18:27.592 --> 00:18:30.605
So, if you compute this alternating sum.

00:18:30.605 --> 00:18:33.879
So, take the total number of
species you've seen exactly once,

00:18:33.879 --> 00:18:37.987
subtract the number of species you've
observed twice, add the number of species

00:18:37.987 --> 00:18:41.700
you've seen three times, subtract
the number you've seen four times.

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:43.840
Compute this alternating sum.

00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:46.780
And this gives an essentially
unbiased estimate

00:18:46.780 --> 00:18:50.140
of the total number of new species
you would expect to discover

00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:53.500
in the same time period if you're
collecting butterflies at the same rate.

00:18:55.300 --> 00:18:58.360
And again, this assumes nothing other
than the fact that you're collecting

00:18:58.360 --> 00:18:59.890
butterflies at the same rate and

00:18:59.890 --> 00:19:02.910
that the underlying distribution
of butterflies is fixed.

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:08.280
So this is pretty surprising right,
we are accurately saying something about

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:10.160
the unobserved portion
of the distribution.

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:15.970
So at roughly the same
time Alan Turing and I.J.

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:19.710
Good were working at Bletchley Park
on the British World War II effort.

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:22.282
They were interested in
estimating similar things.

00:19:22.282 --> 00:19:26.040
So they wanted to estimate,
what's the probability that the next

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:30.150
German enigma machine, ciphertext, it
didn't use ciphertext that we hadn't seen.

00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:36.140
Differently, in Corbet's language, this is
estimating the probability that the next

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:39.780
butterfly that he catches is a new
species that he hasn't seen.

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:43.930
A third way of stating this,

00:19:43.930 --> 00:19:48.380
if you are trying to estimate
the total probability math in the true

00:19:48.380 --> 00:19:52.620
distribution that consistent to mean
elements that you haven't observed yet.

00:19:53.780 --> 00:19:57.960
So this is the missing math in other
words, the math in the true distribution

00:19:57.960 --> 00:20:00.440
which is missing completely from
the empirical distribution.

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:03.770
And it turns out that there's
a very simple estimate for

00:20:03.770 --> 00:20:06.820
this, an essentially unbiased estimate for
this missing math.

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:11.000
And this is now known as the Good–Turing
frequency estimation scheme.

00:20:12.530 --> 00:20:16.160
This says look at the total number of
domain elements that you've seen exactly

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:22.090
once, divide it by your sample size,
and this tells you a good estimate for

00:20:22.090 --> 00:20:25.490
the probability that the next
domain element you see is new.

00:20:25.490 --> 00:20:27.150
So if you think about this.

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:27.950
What does this mean?

00:20:27.950 --> 00:20:32.380
Well, this means, if you want to know,
what's the probability that the next

00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:35.790
song you hear on the radio is a new
song you've never heard before.

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:37.200
Well, think back.

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:40.610
Count the total number of songs you've
heard exactly once in your life.

00:20:40.610 --> 00:20:43.780
Divide that by the number of
songs you've heard, in total.

00:20:43.780 --> 00:20:45.574
And this gives you a estimate for

00:20:45.574 --> 00:20:49.309
the probability you'll be surprised
with the next song you hear.

00:20:49.309 --> 00:20:50.460
Okay and this makes sense.

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:55.424
If in general you were surprised by 3% of
the songs you hear, the probability you're

00:20:55.424 --> 00:20:59.098
going to be surprised by the next
song you hear is probably 3%.

00:21:02.356 --> 00:21:06.947
Okay so Turing and
Fisher give extremely accurate estimates,

00:21:06.947 --> 00:21:12.674
in some sense of a single parameter of
the unseen portion of the distribution.

00:21:12.674 --> 00:21:15.158
So, how much more can
we hope to understand?

00:21:15.158 --> 00:21:19.946
So, in our results we're actually going
to be able to recover estimates of

00:21:19.946 --> 00:21:22.460
the structure of the unseen portions.

00:21:22.460 --> 00:21:26.554
Not just estimate of the total amount of
mass, but actually estimates of the number

00:21:26.554 --> 00:21:29.669
of unseen elements that arise
with different probabilities.

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:34.270
So what is this good for?

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:36.990
Well one can answer questions
in the following form.

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:40.290
One can ask questions like,
what's the value of collecting more data?

00:21:40.290 --> 00:21:42.590
If we were to collect
a hundred times more data,

00:21:43.960 --> 00:21:47.750
how many more domain elements
are we likely to observe?

00:21:47.750 --> 00:21:52.950
Or we could ask questions like, how much
data is necessary before actually we

00:21:52.950 --> 00:21:58.396
are likely to see 99.9% of the phenomena
to rise in the given distribution?

00:21:58.396 --> 00:22:00.775
So this latter question
might be relevant for

00:22:00.775 --> 00:22:04.531
assessing like autonomous vehicles
where you want to know how much more

00:22:04.531 --> 00:22:07.410
do we need to revise these
things until we actually have

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:11.315
a good coverage over the sort of events
that arrive when you're driving?

00:22:14.284 --> 00:22:18.989
And there are a number of more
scientific reasons why you might care

00:22:18.989 --> 00:22:23.275
about understanding the structure
of the unseen portions,

00:22:23.275 --> 00:22:28.332
including understanding properties
of the unseen in humans.

00:22:28.332 --> 00:22:32.730
And I'll give some concrete examples of
that towards the end of this portion.

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:38.150
Okay, so how can we do this?

00:22:38.150 --> 00:22:42.170
Well, let's begin with just a thought
experiment, suppose we actually take 100

00:22:42.170 --> 00:22:46.940
million samples, we draw 100 million
independent draws from some distribution.

00:22:46.940 --> 00:22:49.880
And suppose we see about one
million domain elements, and

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:52.987
each element, each domain element,
we see roughly 100 times.

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:57.040
And if you were to look at
a histogram of the frequencies,

00:22:58.390 --> 00:22:59.425
suppose we see something like this.

00:22:59.425 --> 00:23:02.765
So again, what does this plot represent?

00:23:04.235 --> 00:23:09.605
Well the big spike at 100 represents
the fact that there are lots and lots

00:23:09.605 --> 00:23:14.235
of domain elements that we see roughly
100 times, we see exactly 100 times.

00:23:15.425 --> 00:23:18.415
And yes there's some domain elements
that we only see 80 times or 90 times,

00:23:18.415 --> 00:23:22.155
and some domain elements that we see 120,
or 130 times.

00:23:23.860 --> 00:23:28.560
This is a, you can think of this as
a histogram of the histogram of the data.

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:30.290
So, this is a histogram
of the frequencies.

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:34.140
Suppose someone asks you, well,

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:36.630
what distribution do you think
these samples were drawn from?

00:23:38.540 --> 00:23:42.110
In other words, if someone said hey,
look at this one domain element.

00:23:42.110 --> 00:23:44.464
We observed it 130 times.

00:23:44.464 --> 00:23:48.074
What do you think its true probability is?

00:23:48.074 --> 00:23:49.170
Would you answer?

00:23:49.170 --> 00:23:54.843
Well the empirical probability is
130 divided by the sample size,

00:23:54.843 --> 00:23:57.352
so 130 divided by 100M.

00:23:57.352 --> 00:24:00.983
Or Would you do something else?

00:24:00.983 --> 00:24:05.918
So I claim that actually, based on no
prior assumptions on the distribution,

00:24:05.918 --> 00:24:10.780
only an understanding of the mapping
from a true distribution to an empirical

00:24:10.780 --> 00:24:14.971
distribution, we can accurately
de-noise this distribution,

00:24:14.971 --> 00:24:17.691
de-noise this empirical distribution.

00:24:17.691 --> 00:24:22.117
And conclude that the true distribution
from which these samples were drawn is

00:24:22.117 --> 00:24:26.278
probably extremely close to a uniform
distribution over a million domain

00:24:26.278 --> 00:24:26.977
elements.

00:24:26.977 --> 00:24:28.650
So why is this the case?

00:24:30.040 --> 00:24:33.761
Well, suppose the true distribution
actually were a uniform distribution over

00:24:33.761 --> 00:24:35.084
a million domain elements.

00:24:35.084 --> 00:24:37.786
If we had drawn the 100
million samples from this,

00:24:37.786 --> 00:24:41.730
we would've seen something
extremely similar to this picture.

00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:44.640
Most things we see roughly 100 times,
some things we see more,

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:46.790
some things we see less.

00:24:46.790 --> 00:24:50.980
And the standard deviation and the number
of times we see different elements

00:24:50.980 --> 00:24:53.620
could be roughly ten, and
it would roughly look like this picture.

00:24:55.903 --> 00:25:00.508
If the true distribution had any
significant variance in the probabilities,

00:25:00.508 --> 00:25:02.531
then after you take into account,

00:25:02.531 --> 00:25:07.346
the extra sampling noise that's added just
from the sampling process, I claim we

00:25:07.346 --> 00:25:12.470
would end up seeing this histogram that's
even faster, that has even more variance.

00:25:14.690 --> 00:25:19.330
One way of thinking about this is
that the mapping from the true

00:25:19.330 --> 00:25:22.490
distribution to the empirical distribution

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:25.710
can be almost thought of as
a sort of convolution operator.

00:25:25.710 --> 00:25:30.560
Something that can add noise and
sneers out the probabilities.

00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:33.182
And the question is just to what
extent can you de-convolve?

00:25:33.182 --> 00:25:34.100
Can you de-noise?

00:25:34.100 --> 00:25:37.430
Can you remove the effect
of the sampling noise?

00:25:37.430 --> 00:25:41.420
In this case, I claim you can do it
very robustly and very accurately.

00:25:41.420 --> 00:25:42.720
And what does this mean?

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:44.400
Well, this means that someone comes and
says, hey,

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:48.790
this element was observed 130 times,
what do you think its true probability is?

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:54.490
You can safely say, well, yes,
I know this guy was observed 130 times and

00:25:54.490 --> 00:25:57.340
this other domain element
was only observed 80 times.

00:25:57.340 --> 00:26:01.401
But I think that actually, the true
probabilities of them are probably almost

00:26:01.401 --> 00:26:04.246
the same and
probably equal to just one over a million.

00:26:04.246 --> 00:26:07.548
Just like almost all these other elements,
okay?

00:26:07.548 --> 00:26:10.310
So this is an analog of the reasoning
that we did in the first part.

00:26:13.598 --> 00:26:17.950
Okay, so we can do some de-noising.

00:26:17.950 --> 00:26:20.100
And one question is, well,
how far can we push this?

00:26:23.157 --> 00:26:30.140
So let's just continue trying to
do this kind of mental experiment.

00:26:30.140 --> 00:26:33.710
So suppose we have a histogram based on k
samples, and we see something like this.

00:26:34.790 --> 00:26:39.443
Well, we'd say this looks like maybe it
corresponds to uniform distribution over k

00:26:39.443 --> 00:26:40.761
over 100 elements.

00:26:40.761 --> 00:26:43.218
This is what we just did.

00:26:43.218 --> 00:26:44.175
Why?

00:26:44.175 --> 00:26:47.951
Because if the true distribution had any
significant variance in the probabilities,

00:26:47.951 --> 00:26:50.550
we would expect to see something
slightly from what we see.

00:26:52.530 --> 00:26:57.740
Of course, proving that might be
difficult, but that's the reason.

00:26:59.860 --> 00:27:01.230
Okay, let's look at another example.

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:05.450
Suppose this is actually a histogram
based on 10,000 samples drawn from some

00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:06.110
distribution.

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:09.140
What distribution do you
think this is drawn from?

00:27:10.290 --> 00:27:14.100
Well, you might recognize that this kind
of looks like a Poisson distribution of

00:27:14.100 --> 00:27:15.061
expectation five.

00:27:15.061 --> 00:27:19.944
So you could argue that, well, seeing this
is consistent with having drawn these

00:27:19.944 --> 00:27:24.710
10,000 samples from a uniform
distribution over 2,000 elements.

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:27.030
And in this case you'd be right.

00:27:27.030 --> 00:27:31.750
This histogram was made by
me sampling 10,000 things in

00:27:31.750 --> 00:27:34.180
that lab from a uniform
distribution over 2,000 elements.

00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:36.536
How far can we push this?

00:27:38.950 --> 00:27:42.869
Well, you might recognize that this is
a Poisson distribution of expectation two

00:27:42.869 --> 00:27:46.789
and think that maybe this corresponds to
a uniform distribution over support size

00:27:46.789 --> 00:27:49.651
10,000 divided by 2 over
support size 5,000.

00:27:49.651 --> 00:27:52.225
And you would be correct.

00:27:52.225 --> 00:27:56.223
Maybe this corresponds to a uniform
distribution over 10,000 elements.

00:27:57.820 --> 00:28:00.657
Maybe this is the uniform
distribution over 20,000 elements.

00:28:01.890 --> 00:28:04.387
Maybe this is the uniform
distribution over 50,000 elements.

00:28:06.070 --> 00:28:07.270
So how far can we push this?

00:28:08.590 --> 00:28:12.980
So at this point, what we're claiming
is actually pretty impressive.

00:28:12.980 --> 00:28:17.240
So, we have a histogram,
we have data based on 10,000 samples.

00:28:17.240 --> 00:28:19.650
We've only seen 9,000
distinct domain elements.

00:28:21.160 --> 00:28:25.161
And yet, we're claiming we can reason that
the reason this data was probably drawn

00:28:25.161 --> 00:28:28.551
from some distribution supported
on 50,000 domain elements.

00:28:28.551 --> 00:28:32.453
And that this distribution is
probably extremely close to uniform.

00:28:32.453 --> 00:28:37.019
So we haven't seen most of these domain
elements that we're actually claiming that

00:28:37.019 --> 00:28:40.427
we can accurately describe roughly
how many of them they are and

00:28:40.427 --> 00:28:42.310
roughly their true probability.

00:28:44.530 --> 00:28:48.780
So the natural question is,
well, how general and

00:28:48.780 --> 00:28:52.540
how far can this reasoning be pushed?

00:28:52.540 --> 00:28:56.371
So in all of these examples, we were
thinking about uniform distributions.

00:28:56.371 --> 00:29:00.310
And the only reason we're thinking
about uniform distributions is because

00:29:00.310 --> 00:29:03.030
it's easier to do our mental
calculations on them.

00:29:03.030 --> 00:29:08.540
So what can we say about non-uniform
distributions and how can we actually

00:29:08.540 --> 00:29:13.626
make this approach rigorous and
turn this into an actual algorithm?

00:29:13.626 --> 00:29:18.116
So if you think about what we were doing,
we were basically asking what distribution

00:29:18.116 --> 00:29:22.120
has expected histogram similar to
the observed histogram of the samples.

00:29:24.430 --> 00:29:28.373
And it turns out that you can write this
problem, you can write this problem we

00:29:28.373 --> 00:29:32.089
we're solving in our heads on
the previous slide, as a linear program.

00:29:32.089 --> 00:29:34.826
So I won't explain exactly
what this linear program is.

00:29:34.826 --> 00:29:37.809
But it basically just asks,
find me a distribution with

00:29:37.809 --> 00:29:40.800
expected histogram that's
close to the observed one.

00:29:41.830 --> 00:29:43.900
Why did it end up being a linear program?

00:29:43.900 --> 00:29:46.223
Well, because we only care
about expectations and

00:29:46.223 --> 00:29:48.932
linearity of expectations,
that's where it comes from.

00:29:48.932 --> 00:29:53.408
But I won't go into the details.

00:29:53.408 --> 00:29:55.311
So what does the algorithm do?

00:29:55.311 --> 00:29:58.860
Well, it finds one of these
distributions via linear programming.

00:29:58.860 --> 00:30:02.570
And this linear program basically
just defines a feasible region of

00:30:02.570 --> 00:30:05.980
distributions which are all
consistent with the observation.

00:30:09.820 --> 00:30:12.450
Of course, the technical
challenge is then to argue about

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:16.380
the diameter of this feasible region
shrinks as our sample size grows.

00:30:18.530 --> 00:30:22.752
So we have some concrete technical
results regarding this, which,

00:30:22.752 --> 00:30:25.021
they're tight to constant factor.

00:30:25.021 --> 00:30:28.750
And I just wanted to give you a flavor
of what these sorts of things say.

00:30:28.750 --> 00:30:34.623
So given n independent draws
from some unknown distribution,

00:30:34.623 --> 00:30:39.400
we can recover the sets of probabilities,
p sub i.

00:30:40.780 --> 00:30:45.098
As well as an empirical distribution
would, if the empirical distribution

00:30:45.098 --> 00:30:48.951
would've been based on an extra
logarithmic factor more samples.

00:30:48.951 --> 00:30:50.910
So two points.

00:30:50.910 --> 00:30:54.720
So the first is we are only recovering
the sets of probabilities p sub i.

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:57.332
We're not recovering
the underlying domain.

00:30:57.332 --> 00:30:58.830
So think of it this way.

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:02.060
So suppose you go fishing,
you catch lots of fish.

00:31:02.060 --> 00:31:08.083
We're going to recover a set of
probabilities of the species.

00:31:08.083 --> 00:31:10.362
Although if you haven't caught any tuna,

00:31:10.362 --> 00:31:13.492
I'm not going to say,
there must be a tuna in the ocean.

00:31:13.492 --> 00:31:15.372
Okay, so
I'm not going to label the domain.

00:31:15.372 --> 00:31:20.126
I'm just going to try to recover
the sets of probabilities.

00:31:20.126 --> 00:31:24.733
So one corollary of this result, which is
maybe easier to understand and easier to

00:31:24.733 --> 00:31:29.608
parse, is for a given n independent draws
from some distribution, you can accurately

00:31:29.608 --> 00:31:34.460
estimate the number of new elements
that you would see in a larger sample.

00:31:34.460 --> 00:31:40.720
And you can do this for samples of size
up to O(n log n) in the worst case.

00:31:40.720 --> 00:31:45.190
So in most practical settings,
we seem to be able to

00:31:45.190 --> 00:31:49.330
accurately infer the number of new things
we'll see from much, much larger samples.

00:31:49.330 --> 00:31:52.000
But this is the best we can do
in the worst case sense, and

00:31:52.000 --> 00:31:54.360
there's matching lower information
theoretical lower bound

00:31:56.400 --> 00:32:00.440
I wanted to give a quick
practical application of this.

00:32:00.440 --> 00:32:04.396
So this is in joint work with James Zou
and this appeared Nature Communications.

00:32:04.396 --> 00:32:09.276
So currently we have 60,000 genomes
of healthy individuals that

00:32:09.276 --> 00:32:11.800
have been sequenced.

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:16.760
So one natural question is, what's
the value in sequencing larger cohorts?

00:32:16.760 --> 00:32:19.808
So say we sequence half a million
a people, two million people,

00:32:19.808 --> 00:32:22.340
ten million people,
what's the value in this?

00:32:22.340 --> 00:32:26.436
How many more new medically relevant
mutations are we likely to see if

00:32:26.436 --> 00:32:27.998
we sequence more people?

00:32:31.096 --> 00:32:38.120
So applying the linear programming
based algorithm from two slides ago,

00:32:38.120 --> 00:32:43.210
we can do the following
sort of validation.

00:32:43.210 --> 00:32:45.723
So yes, we have 60,000 genomes
of healthy individuals,

00:32:45.723 --> 00:32:50.470
suppose we only looked at 6,000 of them.

00:32:50.470 --> 00:32:54.631
And based on those 6,000 we tried to
estimate how many new mutations of

00:32:54.631 --> 00:32:58.860
a certain medically relevant type we
would be likely to see if we actually had

00:32:58.860 --> 00:33:00.264
60,000 genomes.

00:33:00.264 --> 00:33:05.184
So based on the first 6,000 genomes we
can predict how many new things we would

00:33:05.184 --> 00:33:07.190
have seen, given more genomes.

00:33:07.190 --> 00:33:10.340
And the blue line is our predictions.

00:33:10.340 --> 00:33:15.076
The red line is the ground truth, because
we do actually have 60,000 genomes.

00:33:15.076 --> 00:33:19.020
And then the light blue area
corresponds to one standard deviation

00:33:19.020 --> 00:33:24.390
over different choices of the 6,000
size cohort out of the 60,000 genomes.

00:33:24.390 --> 00:33:25.970
So we're doing quite well.

00:33:27.480 --> 00:33:31.704
So then the fun part is to say well,
we do actually have 60,000 genomes,

00:33:31.704 --> 00:33:35.480
what would we be likely to see,
if we sequenced a quarter of a million or

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:37.026
half a million more people?

00:33:40.020 --> 00:33:43.260
So just to provide a tiny bit
of background on this, so

00:33:43.260 --> 00:33:46.649
here we are looking at
loss-of-function mutations.

00:33:46.649 --> 00:33:52.110
So a loss-of-function mutation is a
mutation which will break that given gene.

00:33:53.580 --> 00:33:56.610
So on average, most healthy
people have maybe one or one and

00:33:56.610 --> 00:34:00.990
half loss-of- function mutations,
and it's not a big deal.

00:34:00.990 --> 00:34:05.500
So one thing people are doing at the
Stanford Hospital now, if you come in and

00:34:05.500 --> 00:34:10.030
you're very sick, it's usually children
who have very strange symptoms and

00:34:10.030 --> 00:34:12.020
it's not clear what's wrong with them.

00:34:14.210 --> 00:34:17.340
The people at the hospital start by
just sequencing their genome and

00:34:17.340 --> 00:34:19.228
looking for loss-of-function mutations.

00:34:19.228 --> 00:34:23.642
So they ask are there
any mutations which we

00:34:23.642 --> 00:34:28.670
know break a given gene in this patient?

00:34:28.670 --> 00:34:29.390
And then what do they do?

00:34:29.390 --> 00:34:35.282
For each of these broken genes, they ask,
in this cohort of 60,000 healthy

00:34:35.282 --> 00:34:40.080
individuals, were there any healthy
individuals who also had this broken gene?

00:34:41.350 --> 00:34:42.030
And if the answer is yes,

00:34:42.030 --> 00:34:46.010
there are lots of healthy people that
also has this broken gene, then they say,

00:34:46.010 --> 00:34:50.150
well this broken gene probably isn't
causing this, isn't causing them sickness.

00:34:51.350 --> 00:34:54.050
If none of the healthy people
have this broken gene,

00:34:54.050 --> 00:34:59.100
then they say maybe this broken gene
is what's causing the sickness and

00:34:59.100 --> 00:35:01.066
then they try to figure out
what does this gene do?

00:35:01.066 --> 00:35:04.730
Maybe it codes for some protein,
maybe you can synthesize this protein and

00:35:04.730 --> 00:35:07.590
inject it into the patient, and
then the patient gets better.

00:35:09.310 --> 00:35:12.003
Of course we have 20,000 genes,

00:35:12.003 --> 00:35:17.070
we only have 60,000 genomes
healthy people's genomes.

00:35:17.070 --> 00:35:21.574
So there are lots of genes which might be
okay to break which we haven't observed.

00:35:21.574 --> 00:35:26.300
And this plot basically tells us how many
more individuals would we need to sequence

00:35:26.300 --> 00:35:29.510
in other to get a pretty good
coverage over the genes for

00:35:29.510 --> 00:35:32.459
which it's okay to have
broken in a healthy person.

00:35:34.660 --> 00:35:37.580
So this is a real value setting and

00:35:37.580 --> 00:35:40.630
we're going to be trying to
estimate the covariance matrix.

00:35:40.630 --> 00:35:45.136
And this is joint work with Weihao Kong.

00:35:48.932 --> 00:35:52.590
So suppose we have an n independent draws
from some d dimensional distribution.

00:35:53.810 --> 00:35:58.130
A natural question is to understand how
large must n be, what must our sample

00:35:58.130 --> 00:36:01.270
size be, in order to understand
the structure of the distribution?

00:36:02.960 --> 00:36:06.840
And one question is, well,
if we have too little data to actually

00:36:06.840 --> 00:36:11.740
find the structure, then maybe we're not
going to be given enough information to

00:36:11.740 --> 00:36:15.700
accurately return top principal components
or eigenvectors of our distribution.

00:36:16.770 --> 00:36:19.980
Can we still say whether or not there
is structure in the distribution?

00:36:21.310 --> 00:36:25.170
So can you say something like listen, I
don't have enough distribution to, sorry,

00:36:25.170 --> 00:36:30.019
I don't have enough data,
To tell you which

00:36:30.019 --> 00:36:34.709
low dimensional space your
distribution lies in,

00:36:34.709 --> 00:36:40.224
though I do know that it lies
in some low dimensional space.

00:36:40.224 --> 00:36:44.654
Okay, so
this is kind of a thought experiment.

00:36:44.654 --> 00:36:49.428
Suppose I have my data consisting
of 1,000 independent draws from

00:36:49.428 --> 00:36:53.180
some 1,000 dimensional
Gaussian distribution.

00:36:54.900 --> 00:36:59.310
And the Gaussian distribution
has mean zero, and

00:36:59.310 --> 00:37:00.620
I'm not sure what the covariance is.

00:37:02.480 --> 00:37:05.520
And if you were to look at
a histogram of the eigenvalues,

00:37:05.520 --> 00:37:10.550
of the empirical covariance of the data,
so, again you have a 1,000 data points,

00:37:10.550 --> 00:37:14.330
you just look at the covariance
of these data and you look and

00:37:14.330 --> 00:37:18.210
you ask how many directions are there that
look like there are lots of variance?

00:37:18.210 --> 00:37:21.820
And you plot this histogram,
suppose this is what you see.

00:37:21.820 --> 00:37:23.500
So what does this mean?

00:37:23.500 --> 00:37:27.310
This means that well, there are lots of
directions that have almost no variance,

00:37:27.310 --> 00:37:29.150
that's the big spike at the left.

00:37:30.240 --> 00:37:34.825
And there are a few directions that
have variants four and three and so on.

00:37:34.825 --> 00:37:38.600
Okay, so you have your data,
it looks like there's lots of directions.

00:37:38.600 --> 00:37:41.750
It looks like there are 200
directions with very little variance,

00:37:41.750 --> 00:37:43.230
a couple of directions with big variance.

00:37:43.230 --> 00:37:46.000
And now suppose someone asks you,

00:37:47.140 --> 00:37:51.370
what was the true covariance structure
of this underlying distribution?

00:37:53.599 --> 00:37:58.770
Well, I claim that in this case the
empirical eigenvalues are very misleading.

00:37:59.850 --> 00:38:03.780
And in fact, the data was
generated by taking 1,000 samples

00:38:03.780 --> 00:38:05.550
from an identity covariance Gaussian.

00:38:05.550 --> 00:38:08.700
So this means that all of
the directions have the same variance.

00:38:10.240 --> 00:38:15.570
However, because we only had 1,000 samples
from our 1,000 dimensional distribution,

00:38:15.570 --> 00:38:18.050
just due to the randomness,
it looks like there's

00:38:18.050 --> 00:38:20.719
some directions with big variance and
some some directions with small variance.

00:38:22.650 --> 00:38:24.300
If you were to run this experiment again,

00:38:24.300 --> 00:38:28.870
if you were to take another thousand
samples from the same Gaussian, and plot

00:38:28.870 --> 00:38:33.460
the eigenvalues of empirical covariance,
you would get this exact same plot again.

00:38:34.920 --> 00:38:39.498
And if I kept doing this you would keep
getting a plot very similar to this.

00:38:39.498 --> 00:38:44.041
So now, suppose a year later,
some doctor came up to you and said hey,

00:38:44.041 --> 00:38:45.750
I got this new data.

00:38:45.750 --> 00:38:50.386
I'm looking at the covariance structure in

00:38:50.386 --> 00:38:55.260
varied mutations or
some medically important something or

00:38:55.260 --> 00:38:57.400
other, and
this is my covariance structure.

00:38:57.400 --> 00:38:59.520
Look, there are a bunch of interesting,

00:39:00.720 --> 00:39:03.340
there are a bunch of interesting
relationships that have this big variance.

00:39:04.400 --> 00:39:05.030
What would you say?

00:39:06.250 --> 00:39:09.790
You say I remember this spot,
I know this curve.

00:39:09.790 --> 00:39:15.250
This curve actually is the curve that
arises when you have no structure,

00:39:15.250 --> 00:39:17.345
there are no interesting
relationships in the data,

00:39:17.345 --> 00:39:19.310
you just have too small of a sample size.

00:39:22.021 --> 00:39:27.057
So I claim that every time you see a plot
of this, a plot that looks like this,

00:39:27.057 --> 00:39:32.417
reflecting the eigenvalues of an empirical
programming matrix, you can safely

00:39:32.417 --> 00:39:37.805
conclude that actually there's no
structure in the underlying distribution.

00:39:37.805 --> 00:39:42.285
Okay, so one question is, suppose you
actually do look at this histogram of

00:39:42.285 --> 00:39:47.340
eigenvalues of an empirical covariance and
it looks something different than this.

00:39:47.340 --> 00:39:52.300
To what extent can you then de-noise
this and reveal the true covariant

00:39:52.300 --> 00:39:57.730
structure of the distribution and
while you can apply.

00:39:57.730 --> 00:39:59.690
The same mindset as we did earlier,

00:39:59.690 --> 00:40:02.880
but using very different techniques
to say things about this.

00:40:02.880 --> 00:40:04.270
So maybe I'll skip the formal.

00:40:08.653 --> 00:40:10.571
Yeah, I'll skip the formal statement.

00:40:12.064 --> 00:40:14.750
So, just to conclude with
a few final thoughts.

00:40:14.750 --> 00:40:18.840
So, we discussed two, and
maybe three different settings.

00:40:18.840 --> 00:40:23.500
And in all three of these settings,
the underlying approaches are following.

00:40:23.500 --> 00:40:27.140
We first understood the mapping
from the underlying distribution to

00:40:27.140 --> 00:40:28.420
the empirical distribution.

00:40:30.390 --> 00:40:35.700
And then the kind of crux of
what we were actually doing,

00:40:35.700 --> 00:40:37.900
which is try to invert this mapping.

00:40:37.900 --> 00:40:41.920
To say suppose we actually saw
this empirical distribution,

00:40:41.920 --> 00:40:44.990
what does this tell us about
the true distribution underlying it?

00:40:44.990 --> 00:40:50.140
So we're inverting this kind of mapping
from the truth to the empirical.

00:40:51.300 --> 00:40:55.172
And in these three different settings,
it yielded, in some sense,

00:40:55.172 --> 00:40:59.394
pretty surprising results that actually
lead to meaningful algorithms.

00:40:59.394 --> 00:41:04.726
Okay so if you get nothing else from
this talk, I hope you at least remember

00:41:04.726 --> 00:41:10.746
that well in many settings, even when
the empirical distribution is misleading,

00:41:10.746 --> 00:41:15.570
you can still often extract
surprisingly accurate information.

00:41:17.020 --> 00:41:20.560
And the second point that there
are many extremely basic settings.

00:41:20.560 --> 00:41:23.930
Settings that you would have thought
statisticians maybe might have

00:41:23.930 --> 00:41:28.110
understood a century ago,
that we still don't understand.

00:41:28.110 --> 00:41:33.150
So there's much room for future research
about this intersection of algorithms and

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:35.760
statistics and
information theory in learning.

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:36.530
So thank you.

00:41:36.530 --> 00:41:40.090
So I guess now maybe we'll open it up for
questions.

00:41:41.510 --> 00:41:45.110
So I guess there are a few
questions that I wanted to address.

00:41:45.110 --> 00:41:47.883
So there was one question from Andre Coke,

00:41:47.883 --> 00:41:52.437
who asked how do you know that
the unseen distribution law is uniform?

00:41:52.437 --> 00:41:57.620
So this corresponds to
the second portion of the talk.

00:41:57.620 --> 00:42:01.050
This was where we did
this thought experiment.

00:42:01.050 --> 00:42:04.780
Starting with, suppose you have 100
million samples, and you happen to have

00:42:04.780 --> 00:42:09.270
seen roughly 1 million domain elements,
each observed roughly 100 times.

00:42:10.410 --> 00:42:13.596
So, in this kind of thought experiment,

00:42:13.596 --> 00:42:18.339
it did turn out that the unseen
distribution law was uniform.

00:42:18.339 --> 00:42:20.948
So all of these were actually
uniform distributions.

00:42:20.948 --> 00:42:26.150
And so the question is,
how do we know this?

00:42:26.150 --> 00:42:31.030
And well, the claim is,
if your work has [INAUDIBLE] that actually

00:42:31.030 --> 00:42:36.080
looked like this data that I
was hypothetically giving, you

00:42:36.080 --> 00:42:39.570
can actually robustly conclude that the
true underlying distribution is uniform.

00:42:41.030 --> 00:42:43.550
The results apply much more generally.

00:42:43.550 --> 00:42:46.950
So even if the true underlying
distribution isn't uniform,

00:42:46.950 --> 00:42:51.140
this linear programming approach
to inferring the underlying

00:42:51.140 --> 00:42:55.880
structure will actually give you, or
will accurately tell you the structure.

00:42:55.880 --> 00:42:58.950
Even if the underlying
distribution is not uniform.

00:42:58.950 --> 00:43:04.310
The point in these examples was more
just to give a concrete example that's

00:43:04.310 --> 00:43:07.900
somehow simple enough that we
can actually think about it.

00:43:07.900 --> 00:43:10.380
But the underlying techniques
work more generally.

00:43:11.470 --> 00:43:17.280
And this is actually a similar response
applies to some of the earlier

00:43:17.280 --> 00:43:22.280
questions about the kind of warm up,
the first part at the talk which asks,

00:43:22.280 --> 00:43:27.010
are we assuming that the individuals
are identical and independent?

00:43:27.010 --> 00:43:31.020
The answer is, well we're assuming
they're independent, but not identical.

00:43:31.020 --> 00:43:35.050
So maybe some of the people
have larger probabilities,

00:43:35.050 --> 00:43:37.450
some of them have smaller probabilities.

00:43:37.450 --> 00:43:43.700
And we would actually be able to infer
the underlying set of these probabilities,

00:43:43.700 --> 00:43:46.350
even if they are not all the same.

00:43:46.350 --> 00:43:51.373
And when I say we can infer the set of
probabilities, again, to start with,

00:43:51.373 --> 00:43:55.469
we're not trying to figure out
each person's probability.

00:43:55.469 --> 00:43:59.902
So I don't want to know,
if someone just tosses a coin twice,

00:43:59.902 --> 00:44:03.930
I maybe will never know what
their true probability is.

00:44:03.930 --> 00:44:09.220
But I'll infer, roughly, how many people
have probabilities 0.4, how many people

00:44:09.220 --> 00:44:12.934
have probability of roughly 0.5, how many
people have a probability of roughly 0.6.

00:44:14.746 --> 00:44:21.558
Okay, so I guess one other question,
that's actually a really,

00:44:21.558 --> 00:44:26.070
really nice question, from Yan Gokowski.

00:44:26.070 --> 00:44:30.820
So they ask well, what, if any, is the
relationship between these techniques, and

00:44:30.820 --> 00:44:33.880
deconvolution approaches,
such as used in signal processing?

00:44:36.210 --> 00:44:37.530
So this is a very good question.

00:44:40.457 --> 00:44:45.093
If you think about what the mapping is
between kind of a true distribution and

00:44:45.093 --> 00:44:46.978
the empirical distribution,

00:44:46.978 --> 00:44:51.472
in many cases this does look like
you're convolving the true structure of

00:44:51.472 --> 00:44:56.350
the distribution by some noise operator
to yield the empirical distribution.

00:44:57.420 --> 00:45:03.460
So for example, if we have a uniform
distribution over a million elements and

00:45:03.460 --> 00:45:05.760
then we take 100 million
samples from this,

00:45:06.840 --> 00:45:11.870
the empirical frequencies will kind of
look like we convolved this big spike

00:45:13.230 --> 00:45:17.510
of uniform distribution by some
sort of Gaussian-type distribution.

00:45:18.640 --> 00:45:23.700
And in that sense,
you can almost view these techniques

00:45:23.700 --> 00:45:29.634
as deconvolving to remove
the effects of sampling, basically.

00:45:29.634 --> 00:45:34.320
From a technical perspective,
this is actually a little different.

00:45:34.320 --> 00:45:36.320
So usually when you
think of convolution and

00:45:36.320 --> 00:45:40.120
deconvolution, you have a fixed kernel.

00:45:40.120 --> 00:45:44.725
In this case the mapping from the true
distribution to the empirical

00:45:44.725 --> 00:45:48.201
distribution, it's not
actually convolusion,

00:45:48.201 --> 00:45:53.208
even though it's some kind of blurring
operator, but you have a much more

00:45:53.208 --> 00:45:58.581
complicated blurrning operator which
makes it in general harder to deconvolve.

00:45:58.581 --> 00:46:03.278
So, particularly in the last setting
that I had discussed where you have

00:46:03.278 --> 00:46:08.806
some distribution of empirical Eigen
values of the covariance matrix.

00:46:08.806 --> 00:46:13.650
It becomes very clear that the mapping
from the true structure of the geometric

00:46:13.650 --> 00:46:18.720
distribution, the true Eigen values and
the covariance, the empirical thing.

00:46:18.720 --> 00:46:22.810
It's not just a convolution, it's some
very strange kind of noise operator that

00:46:22.810 --> 00:46:26.830
behaves very differently for
the big Eigen vectors and the small ones.

00:46:26.830 --> 00:46:32.370
And so that's where kind of
the technical difficulty comes in.

00:46:32.370 --> 00:46:36.120
That intuitively you want to do
this denoising, deconvolution,

00:46:36.120 --> 00:46:39.100
but it's not actually
a clean deconvolution.

00:46:40.150 --> 00:46:45.180
Okay so, sorry, so the one of
the questions asked, do I have an example

00:46:45.180 --> 00:46:50.010
of eigenvalue distribution for covariance
matrix with meaningful structure.

00:46:50.010 --> 00:46:52.240
So I do have some thoughts I can show.

00:46:54.000 --> 00:46:59.470
So each of these plots corresponds to the,
you can think of this as

00:46:59.470 --> 00:47:05.580
the cumulative distribution function
of the eigenvalues of a distribution.

00:47:05.580 --> 00:47:07.070
So what does this actually mean?

00:47:07.070 --> 00:47:12.610
So the blue line is corresponding
to the true distribution.

00:47:12.610 --> 00:47:17.470
And this means that well half of the
eigenvalues are one of the covariants and

00:47:17.470 --> 00:47:18.260
half of them are two.

00:47:19.350 --> 00:47:20.180
What does this mean?

00:47:20.180 --> 00:47:24.540
This means that we have some
high dimensional distribution.

00:47:24.540 --> 00:47:28.880
And half of the direction to variance one,
half of the direction to variance two.

00:47:30.890 --> 00:47:34.717
If you were to look at
the empirical covariance matrix.

00:47:34.717 --> 00:47:38.627
The very light blue lines
that look like these curves,

00:47:38.627 --> 00:47:43.732
this represents the eigenvalues of
the empirical covariance matrix.

00:47:43.732 --> 00:47:48.607
So in particular if you have relatively
few samples, the directions of variance 1,

00:47:48.607 --> 00:47:53.076
the directions of variance 2 all just
get blurred together and it looks like

00:47:53.076 --> 00:47:57.910
some directions to variance 1.5,
some directions to variance 0.3 and so on.

00:47:59.300 --> 00:48:05.170
Using this sort of, I guess you can almost
think of it as a de-convolution approach.

00:48:05.170 --> 00:48:09.910
Even when the number of samples is linear
or slightly smaller than the dimension,

00:48:09.910 --> 00:48:16.255
we can actually recover the underlying
structure, namely that there are half

00:48:16.255 --> 00:48:21.295
the dimensions have variance one then
half the dimensions have variance two.

00:48:21.295 --> 00:48:26.381
And we have other examples
of cases like this

00:48:26.381 --> 00:48:34.915
where there is actually structure in
the underlying covariance matrix.

00:48:34.915 --> 00:48:38.722
&gt;&gt; So maybe, Greg,
now that we're at the top of the hour,

00:48:38.722 --> 00:48:40.918
you can take one more question?

00:48:40.918 --> 00:48:42.376
&gt;&gt; Okay, so, yeah, so

00:48:42.376 --> 00:48:46.926
I guess I'll finish with a slightly
more kind of general question.

00:48:46.926 --> 00:48:49.733
So this question's from
Orlando Tucker who asked,

00:48:49.733 --> 00:48:53.770
how important is this information in
machine learning and deep learning?

00:48:53.770 --> 00:48:54.977
So this is a good question.

00:48:54.977 --> 00:48:59.414
So most of the work I do kind of
focuses on fundamental kind of

00:48:59.414 --> 00:49:03.322
questions that have more
of a theoretical nature.

00:49:03.322 --> 00:49:09.671
And I guess my answer

00:49:09.671 --> 00:49:13.784
is twofold.

00:49:13.784 --> 00:49:18.392
So looking forward, at some point,
I do think if we want progress in machine

00:49:18.392 --> 00:49:23.576
learning, deep learning to continue at the
rate that it has, we will actually need to

00:49:23.576 --> 00:49:28.626
start thinking about how do you get more
information from the data than currently.

00:49:28.626 --> 00:49:33.696
So deep learning has done a lot of great
things, but it is extremely data hungry.

00:49:33.696 --> 00:49:38.200
So you really do need to
give it a lot of data.

00:49:38.200 --> 00:49:43.034
And if you think about it, what's the
chance that we're going to get an extra

00:49:43.034 --> 00:49:46.883
factor of 1,000 to 10,000
more good text data?

00:49:46.883 --> 00:49:50.252
Not great.
We already have all of Wikipedia,

00:49:50.252 --> 00:49:55.006
finding an extra factor
of 10,000 more good data,

00:49:55.006 --> 00:49:59.178
it doesn't seem like we
should go down that path.

00:49:59.178 --> 00:50:03.760
Instead it seems like we should try to
figure out how to get more from the data

00:50:03.760 --> 00:50:04.360
we have.

00:50:04.360 --> 00:50:07.845
I guess from a very concrete standpoint,
so

00:50:07.845 --> 00:50:13.495
one can plug in some of these techniques
kind of below the deep learning or

00:50:13.495 --> 00:50:17.937
more high level machine
learning portions of a system.

00:50:17.937 --> 00:50:23.367
So one can these sorts of techniques
to come out correct empirical

00:50:23.367 --> 00:50:30.092
distributions a little bit and then hand
them off to these higher level systems.

00:50:30.092 --> 00:50:35.348
And also I guess one dimension that there
is, yes there's a lot of kind of higher

00:50:35.348 --> 00:50:40.762
level machine learning stuff, but there's
also need to actually concretely get

00:50:40.762 --> 00:50:45.963
information about the value of collecting
more data and questions like this.

00:50:45.963 --> 00:50:51.781
So one thing I'm starting to think about
is how do you use these ideas together

00:50:51.781 --> 00:50:57.867
with higher level machine learning things
to ask if you were to take ten times more

00:50:57.867 --> 00:51:03.796
data how would the performance of your
systems improve and that sort of thing.

00:51:03.796 --> 00:51:08.680
So at least some of the ideas can
work nicely in conjunction with

00:51:08.680 --> 00:51:13.023
these machine learning and
deep learning approaches.

00:51:13.023 --> 00:51:14.586
So thank you.

00:51:14.586 --> 00:51:17.662
&gt;&gt; Great, so thank you, Greg for
a wonderful presentation and

00:51:17.662 --> 00:51:20.629
for taking time to answer some
of our attendee questions.

00:51:20.629 --> 00:51:23.108
And we want to thank you for
taking the time to attend today.

00:51:23.108 --> 00:51:25.238
And hopefully we'll see
you at our next webinar.

00:51:25.238 --> 00:51:26.077
Have a great day.

